  "title": "Schizophrenia predicted effector genes (Trubetskoy V et al. 2022)",
  "bodyHtml": "<p>This table displays results from the study:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Mapping genomic loci implicates genes and synaptic biology in schizophrenia.<br />\r\nTrubetskoy V, Pardiñas AF, <i>et al.</i><br />\r\nNature. 2022 Apr;604(7906):502-508. doi: 10.1038/s41586-022-04434-5.<br />\r\nPMID: <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/35396580\" target=\"_blank\"> 35396580</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The authors performed a two-stage multi-ancestry genome-wide association study of up to 76,755 individuals with schizophrenia and 243,649 control individuals and then generated supporting evidence types to predict effector genes at the GWAS significant loci. Mouse over the column headers for more details about each evidence type.</p>\r\n",
